1. The comic
------------

Juan Salvo, the cascarudos, the deadly snowfall and the other characters, creatures, names and story elements of the
comic by Héctor Germán Oesterheld (script) and Francisco Solano López (art), first published in Hora Cero Semanal
(Editorial Frontera, Buenos Aires, 1957–1959), are used in this game with the permission of their rights holders.
They are NOT covered by the MIT licence or by CC BY 4.0, and nothing in this repository grants any right to them. To
use them in anything else, ask their rights holders.

The comic's title, El Eternauta, is a trademark of its rights holders. This project names it only to say which comic
the game is inspired by, with their permission; it is not the game's name. "Hora Cero" is the name of the magazine in
which the comic first appeared. This repository licenses no name or trademark: the MIT licence and CC BY 4.0 cover
code and assets, not the game's name, its logo or the comic's names.


2. Santiago Siri's licensed models (included, NOT under MIT or CC BY)
---------------------------------------------------------------------

The 3D models of Juan Salvo, of the cascarudo and of the Plaza de Mayo's vehicles were made by Santiago Siri and are
included in this repository with the author's permission, for use with Hora Cero. They are NOT covered by the MIT
licence or by CC BY 4.0: all rights are reserved by their author, and nothing in this repository grants any right to
use them outside Hora Cero; ask the author. Each folder has a CREDITS.txt.

  - godot/art_pack/juan_suit/: the blue-suit survivor (juan_suit.glb, on a 61-bone skeleton, with its colour,
    normal and metal-roughness maps), made ready for the game from its original file (not in this repository) by
    tools/juan_suit.py; the game's Juan Salvo on 20-21 October 2026 and, since 21 October, the insulating suit a
    neighbour finds in the truck on Yrigoyen. The rifle in his hands (art/juan_salvo/source/rifle.glb) comes from
    Alejandro Vizio's Juan Salvo and is under these same terms;
  - godot/art_pack/cascarudo/: the 3D model of the cascarudo (cascarudo.glb), its shape as made, with a skeleton for
    its legs added by tools/art_pack.py rig-creature (tools/creature_rig.py), and its lighter copy (cascarudo_lod.glb,
    decimated from it and rigged alike);
  - godot/icon.png: the game's icon, a render of the cascarudo's model (tools/dev/creature_icon.gd);
  (godot/art_pack/juan_salvo/, the game's Juan Salvo, is NOT one of these: it is Alejandro Vizio's own model, made
  from photographs of a friend's figure, its head, skin and eyes from MakeHuman's CC0 base, his portrait painted for
  it, and it is under LICENSE-ASSETS like the rest of the game's own art;)
  - docs/design/: the PNG exports of the Figma design kit; what they show of Juan Salvo, the cascarudos, the vehicles
    and the icon is under these same terms, and the rest follows LICENSE-ASSETS like the files it shows;
  - art/vehicles/: the Plaza de Mayo's vehicles as delivered (the Línea 60 colectivo, colectivo60_lod1.glb, and its
    lettering, colectivo60_lettering.png, with the operator's name and the route boards as made; two cars of the
    Retro Vehicle Pack, models/07_microcar.glb and models/08_work_pickup.glb, and the pack's generator,
    source/build_vehicles.py), and six cars of the 1940s and 50s made for Hora Cero on that generator
    (source/build_vehicles_1950s.py and the models it writes, models/1*_*.glb, models/manifest_1950s.json), under
    the same terms;
  - godot/assets/vehicles/: the game-ready meshes made from those sources by tools/vehicles.gd (*.res) and the
    lettering texture scaled down; godot/assets/vehicles/CREDITS.txt lists what was changed.

What was made from these models for Hora Cero (the game-ready Juan Salvo, the cascarudo's rig, the icon, the six
cars, the game-ready meshes) is under the same terms as the models: not MIT, not CC BY. The scripts that do it
(tools/juan_suit.py, tools/person_refine.py, tools/creature_rig.py, tools/vehicles.gd, tools/dev/*) are code, under
MIT, except the vehicle generators in art/vehicles/source/.

Juan Salvo and the cascarudos are, besides, characters and creatures of the comic (section 1), used with the
permission of its rights holders.

The game does not need these files to run. A build without them shows neutral stand-ins (a grey mannequin and a grey
block, each with a LICENSED ART PENDING plate) and the level kit's own unbranded colectivo and cars; README.md says how
(delete the folders and empty the two slots' file, portrait and lod in godot/art_pack/manifest.json).


